"gloss":"held"}],"gloss":"held"},{"surface":"வேலே","parts":[{"text":"வேலே","lemma":"வேல்","gloss":"spear indeed"}],"gloss":"spear indeed"}]}],"literal":"While drums beat and white conches roar / Pāri, the lord of Parampu hill where roaring white waterfalls flow dashing against rocks on the long mountain range fought by kings / and Ōri of the strong bow who ruled the lofty Kolli hills / and Malaiyaṉ of valiant war and rain-like generosity who rode Kāri and conquered in the great battle / and Eḻiṉi of curved ornaments and wood-apple garland who held a sharp spear and rode no horse / and Pēkaṉ, the lord of the great mountain with high peaks protected by deities of rare power where darkness abides in deep caves on cool, moist slopes / and Āy, sung by Mōci of refined words / and Naḷḷai of abundantly excellent generosity who gave without failing to end the great distress of those who came thinking of him, and chased away enemies / after these seven patrons passed away, causing distress to come / those who come singing and others gathered / saying 'let us remove the poverty of the needy', I have hastened and come here thinking of you / O Lord of Mutiram hill of unwavering fresh wealth, where on the mountain slope where bamboo grows tall along with laurel trees, a male monkey having obtained a ripe, thorny jackfruit from the beautiful wild jackfruit tree calls out to its soft-headed female monkey, beckoning with its hand / O Kumaṇa of the well-made chariot and excellence shining here / with generosity risen above fame / let the spear you hold prevail over enemies!","translation":"While drums beat and white conches roar, the seven great patrons of old—Pāri of the Parampu hills where white waterfalls dash against rocks; Ōri of the strong bow who ruled the Kolli hills; Malaiyaṉ of rain-like generosity who rode Kāri to victory; Eḻiṉi of the sharp spear and wood-apple garland; Pēkaṉ of the cool, deity-guarded mountain peaks; Āy, praised in refined words by the poet Mōci; and Naḷḷai who chased away enemies and gave unstintingly to the distressed—have all passed away. In the grief of their absence, those who sing and others have gathered, saying, 'Who will now relieve the poverty of the needy?' Thinking of you, I have hastened here. O Lord of the prosperous Mutiram hills, where on bamboo-covered slopes a male monkey, having found a ripe, thorny jackfruit, beckons with his hand to call his soft-headed mate! O Kumaṇa of the splendid chariot, whose fame and generosity shine bright, may the spear you hold always triumph over your enemies!","modern":"முரசுகள் முழங்கவும், வெண்சங்குகள் ஒலிக்கவும், மூவேந்தர்களுடன் போரிட்ட பெருமைக்குரிய நீண்ட மலைகளிலிருந்து வெள்ளிய அருவிகள் பாறைகளில் மோதி விழும் பறம்பு மலையின் தலைவனான பாரியும்; உயர்ந்த கொல்லி மலையை ஆண்ட வலிய வில்லையுடைய ஓரியும்; காரி என்னும் குதிரையை ஏறிச் சென்று பெரும் போரில் வென்ற, மாரி போன்ற கொடையையும் வீரத்தையும் உடைய மலையனும்; குதிரை ஏறாமல் கையில் ஏந்திய கூர்மையான வேலும் விளாம்பழ மாலையும் வளைந்த அணிகலன்களும் உடைய எழினியும்; குளிர்ந்த மலைச்சாரலின் இருள் சூழ்ந்த குகைகளில் வாழும் தெய்வங்கள் காக்கும் உயர்ந்த சிகரங்களையுடைய பெரிய மலைநாடான பேகனும்; திருந்திய சொற்களால் மோசி பாடிய ஆயும்; தன்னை நாடி வருவோரின் துன்பம் தீரக் குறைவின்றி வாரி வழங்கிய கொடைத்தன்மையும் பகைவரை விரட்டிய வீரமும் உடைய நள்ளையும் ஆகிய இந்த எழுவரும் மறைந்த பின்பு, வறுமையால் வாடும் பாணர்களும் பிறரும் கூடி, 'இரப்போரின் துன்பத்தைத் தீர்ப்பவர் யார்?' என்று வருந்தினர். அதனால், உன்னை நினைத்து நான் விரைந்து இங்கு வந்தேன். மூங்கில்கள் வளரும் மலைச்சாரலில், பலா மரத்தின் முட்கள் நிறைந்த பழுத்த கனியைப் பெற்ற ஆண் குரங்கு, தன் மென்மையான தலையையுடைய பெண் குரங்கைக் கைகாட்டி அழைக்கும் வளமிக்க முதிர மலையின் தலைவனே! விளங்கும் சிறப்பும் தேரும் உடைய குமணனே! புகழோடு விளங்கும் கொடைத்தன்மை கொண்டவனே! நீ ஏந்திய வேல் பகைவரை வென்று மேம்படுவதாக!","explanation":"The poet Peruñcittiraṉār addresses King Kumaṇaṉ in this poem of the pāṭāṇ tiṇai (praise of a patron). He laments the passing of the seven great patrons of the Sangam era (Pāri, Ōri, Malaiyaṉ, Eḻiṉi, Pēkaṉ, Āy, and Naḷḷai), whose deaths left the world of poets and artists in deep distress and poverty. Seeking a savior who can match their legendary generosity, the poet hastens to Kumaṇaṉ, the lord of the fertile Mutiram hills. The poet describes the rich landscape of Mutiram, where a male monkey shares a ripe jackfruit with its mate, symbolizing the abundance of the land and the sharing nature of its ruler. The poem concludes with a blessing for Kumaṇaṉ's victory, wishing that his spear always triumphs over his enemies.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.8],[0.8,0.8],[0.8,1.4],[1.4,2.6],[2.6,3.8],[3.8,4.0],[4.0,4.2],[4.2,4.9],[4.9,6.0],[6.0,6.1],[6.1,6.6],[6.6,6.9],[6.9,7.3],[7.3,8.6],[8.6,8.6],[8.6,9.1],[9.1,9.6],[9.6,9.9],[9.9,11.0],[11.0,11.2],[11.2,11.3],[11.3,11.9],[11.9,12.7],[12.7,12.9],[12.9,13.3],[13.3,14.0],[14.0,15.0],[15.0,15.3],[15.3,15.5],[15.5,16.4],[16.4,17.3],[17.3,17.3],[17.3,17.7],[17.7,18.1],[19.16,19.86],[19.86,19.96],[19.96,20.26],[20.26,20.96],[20.96,22.16],[22.16,22.16],[22.16,22.66],[22.66,22.96],[22.96,23.76],[23.76,24.76],[24.76,24.86],[24.86,25.26],[25.26,26.06],[26.06,27.06],[27.06,27.36],[27.36,27.46],[27.46,27.86],[27.86,28.26],[28.26,29.36],[29.36,29.46],[29.46,29.96],[29.96,30.76],[30.76,31.56],[31.56,31.86],[31.86,32.56],[32.56,32.96],[32.96,33.96],[33.96,33.96],[33.96,34.36],[34.36,34.96],[34.96,35.56],[35.56,35.66],[35.66,36.36],[36.36,36.66],[36.66,36.96],[37.64,38.24],[38.24,38.44],[38.44,38.84],[38.84,39.14],[39.14,39.24],[39.38,39.65],[39.65,40.34],[40.34,40.74],[40.74,41.04],[41.04,42.14],[42.14,42.24],[42.24,42.52],[42.64,43.34],[43.34,43.64],[43.64,44.54],[44.54,44.94],[44.94,45.04],[45.04,46.11],[46.11,46.54],[46.54,46.84],[46.84,47.51],[47.51,47.64],[47.64,48.94],[48.94,49.24],[49.24,49.44],[49.44,50.24],[50.24,50.94],[50.94,51.34],[51.34,51.64],[51.64,51.84],[51.84,53.04],[53.04,53.14],[53.14,53.94],[53.94,54.34],[55.04,55.54],[55.54,55.64],[55.64,56.74],[56.74,56.74],[56.74,57.84],[57.84,58.04],[58.04,58.